npm install mongodb
import { MongoClient, ObjectId } from 'mongodb';
const client = new MongoClient(process.env.MONGO_URI);
await client.connect();
const db = client.db('shop');
const orders = db.collection('orders');
await orders.insertOne({
_id: new ObjectId(),
user_id: 1,
total: 99.0,
status: 'paid',
at: new Date()
});
const recent = await orders
.find({ user_id: 1, status: 'paid' })
.sort({ at: -1 })
.limit(10)
.toArray();
await client.close();
Strengths:
Thin, no opinion.
TypeScript first-class.
Fastest driver in the JS ecosystem.
Combine with zod / valibot for runtime schema validation if you want types without a heavier ORM/ODM.

npm install mongoose
import mongoose, { Schema, model } from 'mongoose';
await mongoose.connect(process.env.MONGO_URI);
const UserSchema = new Schema({
name: { type: String, required: true },
email: { type: String, unique: true, required: true, match: /@/ },
age: { type: Number, min: 0, max: 150 },
tags: [String],
created_at: { type: Date, default: Date.now }
});
UserSchema.methods.greet = function () { return `Hi, ${this.name}`; };
UserSchema.pre('save', function (next) { /* hook */ next(); });
const User = model('User', UserSchema);
const u = await User.create({ name: 'Ada', email: 'ada@x' });
u.greet();
Mongoose adds:
Schemas + types + validation at the app layer.
Pre/post hooks (lifecycle middleware).
Population (ref + populate — $lookup from the app side).
Virtuals + getters/setters.
Methods + statics.
Trade-off: extra abstraction, ~30 % slower than the raw driver, sometimes opaque. Many teams pick the official driver + Zod for new projects in 2026 — leaner.

Prisma (originally Postgres-only) has supported MongoDB since 2022. Schema-first ORM with TypeScript types generated for you.
// schema.prisma
datasource db {
provider = "mongodb"
url = env("DATABASE_URL")
}
model User {
id String @id @default(auto()) @map("_id") @db.ObjectId
name String
email String @unique
age Int?
createdAt DateTime @default(now())
}
import { PrismaClient } from '@prisma/client';
const prisma = new PrismaClient();
const u = await prisma.user.create({ data: { name: 'Ada', email: 'ada@x' } });
const all = await prisma.user.findMany({ where: { age: { gte: 18 } }, orderBy: { createdAt: 'desc' } });
Strengths: type-safe queries (compile-time errors on wrong fields), excellent DX.
Weaknesses (vs Postgres Prisma):
Relations on MongoDB use references-with-IDs (not native foreign keys). Joins via include are app-side.
Some Prisma features are SQL-only.
Choose Prisma if the rest of your stack uses Prisma + you value the TypeScript ergonomics. Choose the official driver if you want raw control or you write a lot of aggregation pipelines (Prisma's aggregation API is less expressive).

