End-to-end model:
// devices — slow-changing dimension
db.devices.insertOne({
_id: "d1",
name: "kitchen-temp",
location: { lat: 51.5, lon: -0.1 },
installed_at: new Date(),
status: "active"
});
// telemetry — time-series collection (Chapter 9)
db.createCollection("telemetry", {
timeseries: { timeField: "ts", metaField: "device_id", granularity: "seconds" },
expireAfterSeconds: 60 * 60 * 24 * 30
});
// rollup collection — pre-aggregated minute / hour / day
db.createCollection("telemetry_5m", {
timeseries: { timeField: "bucket_start", metaField: "device_id", granularity: "minutes" },
expireAfterSeconds: 60 * 60 * 24 * 365
});
Ingest path: device → MQTT broker → ingest service → telemetry.insertMany([...]) in batches.
Rollup: scheduled aggregation every 5 minutes, $merge into telemetry_5m. Old raw data drops at TTL; rollups persist a year.
Real-time queries: vector dashboard reads telemetry_5m for the chart, drills into raw telemetry only when investigating an incident.
Alerting: change stream on telemetry triggers a function when temp > threshold.
This pattern (raw TS + pre-aggregated rollups + dashboards on rollups) is the canonical IoT architecture in 2026.

Collections + relationships:
// products — small enough for catalogue queries
db.products.insertOne({
_id: "p1",
type: "shirt", // polymorphic discriminator
title: "Cotton Tee",
price: 19.99,
stock: 120,
attributes: [ // attribute pattern (Post 07)
{ k: "size", v: "M" },
{ k: "colour", v: "red" }
],
category_path: ["clothing", "tops", "tshirts"] // materialised path
});
// category tree — parent ref + ancestors hybrid
db.categories.insertOne({
_id: "tshirts",
name: "T-shirts",
parent: "tops",
ancestors: ["clothing", "tops"]
});
// users
db.users.insertOne({
_id: 1, name: "Ada", email: "ada@x",
addresses: [{ label: "home", line1: "...", city: "London" }] // 1:few embed
});
// carts — small, per-user, may be soft-deleted
db.carts.insertOne({
_id: 1, user_id: 1, items: [{ p: "p1", qty: 2 }],
updated_at: new Date()
});
// orders — immutable + extended reference for snapshots
db.orders.insertOne({
_id: ObjectId(),
user_id: 1,
user_email_at_order: "ada@x", // extended ref — survives email change
items: [
{ p: "p1", title_at_order: "Cotton Tee", price_at_order: 19.99, qty: 2 }
],
total: 39.98,
status: "paid",
at: new Date()
});
// reviews — separate collection, queried both ways
db.reviews.insertOne({
product_id: "p1", user_id: 1, rating: 5, body: "..."
});
// audit_log — TTL'd
db.createCollection("audit_log", { capped: false });
db.audit_log.createIndex({ at: 1 }, { expireAfterSeconds: 60 * 60 * 24 * 90 });
Hot paths:
Catalogue search → Atlas Search index on products.
Personalised recommendations → Atlas Vector Search over product embeddings.
Cart updates → single-doc atomic $inc on stock + push to cart.
Order placement → single-doc transaction (Post 09) covering stock decrement + order insert.
Reporting → $merge materialised views (daily_revenue, top_products).

// users — basic profile
db.users.insertOne({ _id: 1, handle: "ada", display_name: "Ada", followers_count: 0, following_count: 0 });
// follows — separate edge collection (many:many)
db.follows.createIndex({ follower: 1, followee: 1 }, { unique: true });
db.follows.createIndex({ followee: 1, since: -1 });
db.follows.insertOne({ follower: 2, followee: 1, since: new Date() });
// posts — immutable + indexed by author + time
db.posts.createIndex({ author: 1, at: -1 });
db.posts.insertOne({ _id: ObjectId(), author: 1, text: "hello", at: new Date(), likes: 0 });
// likes — separate edge
db.likes.createIndex({ user: 1, post: 1 }, { unique: true });
// timeline approaches:
// (A) Fan-out on READ — query at read time
function timeline(userId, before) {
const following = await db.follows.find({ follower: userId }).map(d => d.followee).toArray();
return db.posts.find({
author: { $in: following },
at: { $lt: before }
}).sort({ at: -1 }).limit(20).toArray();
}
// (B) Fan-out on WRITE — push to each follower's inbox at post time
// timeline_inbox: { user_id, post_id, author, at }
// On post: insert one row per follower
// Read: query inbox where user_id = me, sort at desc
Trade-offs:
Read fan-out — cheap writes, expensive reads (especially for users following thousands). Good for small networks.
Write fan-out — cheap reads, expensive writes (a celebrity's post inserts millions of rows). Good for read-heavy feeds.
Hybrid — fan-out on write for normal users; fan-out on read for celebrities (mark with is_high_fanout: true, query separately).
This is the Twitter/Mastodon problem. There's no one-size-fits-all answer.

import { MongoClient, ObjectId } from 'mongodb';
import { z } from 'zod';
// schema validation at app boundary
const Order = z.object({
_id: z.instanceof(ObjectId).optional(),
user_id: z.number().int().positive(),
total: z.number().positive(),
status: z.enum(['pending', 'paid', 'shipped', 'cancelled']),
at: z.date()
});
type Order = z.infer<typeof Order>;
const client = new MongoClient(process.env.MONGO_URI!, {
maxPoolSize: 50,
minPoolSize: 5,
appName: 'orders-svc',
writeConcern: { w: 'majority', j: true, wtimeout: 5000 }
});
await client.connect();
const db = client.db('shop');
const orders = db.collection<Order>('orders');
export async function createOrder(input: Omit<Order, '_id'>) {
const parsed = Order.omit({ _id: true }).parse(input);
const r = await orders.insertOne({ ...parsed, at: new Date() });
return { _id: r.insertedId, ...parsed };
}
export async function findRecentByUser(user_id: number, limit = 20) {
return orders
.find({ user_id, status: 'paid' })
.sort({ at: -1 })
.limit(limit)
.maxTimeMS(500)
.toArray();
}
// graceful shutdown
process.on('SIGTERM', async () => { await client.close(); process.exit(0); });
Recipe summary:
One client per process; explicit pool + concerns + appName.
Validate at the app boundary (Zod) — runtime + types.
.maxTimeMS() on user-facing queries.
Graceful shutdown.
Drop into a Fastify / Express / Hono handler — done.
